diagnosis_text:
A thorough multi-layer investigation of the pc1 → pc2 path (195.11.14.2 → 200.1.1.2) found no fault at any layer.

**Affected component:** None — all subsystems are operating normally.

**Mechanism (why no fault):** The claimed ~33% packet loss has no supporting mechanism in the current topology state. Three categories of injection were checked and ruled out: (1) traffic-control shaping — `tc_snapshot` across all six path interfaces shows only default `fq_codel` qdiscs; no `netem` loss, `tbf` rate-limit, or corruption rule exists. (2) Firewall/ACL drops — `infra_sweep` found zero DROP rules on all four devices, including no iptables `statistic --mode nth` rule that could produce periodic drops. (3) Process-level interference — `pressure_sweep` found no stress tools, flood scripts, or flap processes running on any device.

**Localization:** None. Both routers (router1 in AS 1, router2 in AS 2) have their BGP sessions fully established and are correctly advertising their respective prefixes (195.11.14.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24). No device shows addressing errors, missing routes, or L2 anomalies.

**Confirmation:** A 30-packet ICMP probe from pc1 to pc2 (200.1.1.2) produced 0% loss with stable RTT (~0.91 ms average).

**Root cause:** No anomaly is present; the path is fully healthy end-to-end.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. The reported packet loss is unconfirmed and unsubstantiated by any observable mechanism or trace in the live lab.
=== END ===